Hardware Transactional Memory (HTM)

• Definition: A concurrency control mechanism built directly into the processor silicon (using CPU caches and multi-word synchronization instructions).
• How it works: The CPU tracks read/write sets directly inside hardware caches. If another core touches the same cache line during a transaction, an automatic hardware abort and rollback occur.
• Requirements: Requires explicit chip-level support (such as Intel TSX or IBM Power Architecture TM).
• Pros: Delivers near-native speed and minimal runtime overhead compared to software tracking.
• Cons: "Best-effort" hardware limits mean large transactions can overflow cache capacity and fail, and certain instructions (like I/O or system calls) cannot run inside a hardware transaction.


Software Transactional Memory (STM)

• Definition: A concurrency control scheme implemented entirely in software (through a programming language runtime library or specialized data structures).
• How it works: It uses private read/write journals or logs to track memory accesses. If a conflict is detected when committing the transaction, it rolls back and automatically retries.
• Requirements: Requires minimal hardware support—typically just a basic atomic instruction like Compare-and-Swap (CAS) or Load-Link/Store-Conditional (LL/SC).
• Pros: Highly portable, flexible, and works across any CPU architecture without size or instruction set limits.
• Cons: Incurs noticeable computational and memory overhead from tracking logs and managing validation in software.

DIFF

Feature:	Software Transactional Memory (STM) | Hardware Transazctional Memory (HTM)
Implementation: 	Runtime library or language construct	|   CPU/processor extensions and cache tracking
Performance:	Slower due to software logging overhead	| High speed near-zero baseline overhead
Hardware Dependency:	Low (only needs basic atomic primitives like CAS)   |   High (requires specific CPU microarchitecture support)
Transaction Size Limits:	Flexible (limited only by available memory) |   Rigid (restricted by CPU cache size or architecture rules)
Abort Reasons:	Data conflicts or explicit retry logic	| Data conflicts, cache capacity overflow, or illegal instructions


